{
  "version": 3,
  "title": "What if your plane blew up?",
  "series": "Freefall / Pick Your Landing",
  "narration": "Your plane just blew up. One second you are cruising at thirty thousand feet, the next there is nothing but sky between you and the ground, and no parachute. First thing that happens: the air up here is so thin, there is not enough oxygen, your vision narrows, and you pass out. Cold, dark, done. But here is the weird part. As you fall, the air gets thicker, and around ten thousand feet, you wake back up. Now you are wide awake, tumbling at about a hundred and twenty miles per hour, wind screaming, and you get to pick where this ends. Look down. You have got a river, a forest, a rocky field, and a plowed farm. The river looks soft and inviting. At this speed, water hits like concrete. Trees can break your fall, if you are lucky with the branches. Rocks are just rocks. So you arch your back and steer for the farm. Spread your arms, slow your spin, and aim for the softest ground you can see. Five thousand feet. Two thousand. Five hundred. Feet first, knees bent, and roll. He rolls into the wheat, gets up, brushes off his jacket, and walks away. So, next time, take the train.",
